WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.850
- I recently posted five individual videos

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.610
on how to communicate
clearly and confidently.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.040
What I've done in this video
is to stitch those all together

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.020
so you can go through the whole series,

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.750
step-by-step, in one organized place.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:13.804
Here we go.

00:00:13.804 --> 00:00:16.804
(upbeat rock music)

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.183
The point of this video, part one,

00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:22.350
is that if you want to speak more clearly

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.410
and confidently, then use plain language.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:29.310
Why is that? Because plain
language is clear language.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.840
You'll be at your most confident

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.060
when you know what you're saying.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.360
Tip one, don't use fancy vocabulary words

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.430
for their own sake.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.460
A lot of people don't like
this tip, but it works.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.780
I'll give you some examples in a minute,

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.010
but I wanted to first address

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.170
why we sometimes use fancy words

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.600
when they don't add value.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.320
One reason people use fancy
words is they mistakenly believe

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.330
it'll make them sound smart.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.760
In other words, they are
trying to impress others

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.320
with a big vocabulary.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.050
They might not state that goal aloud.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.720
They might not even realize
that it's their goal,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.210
but it is a common go-to mindset

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.770
that's just below the surface

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.480
and it motivates many of us.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.040
And when we talk to impress others,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.250
we tend to use fancier
words than necessary.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.030
We stretch to the outer
limits of our vocabulary,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.310
but we may find that those fancy words

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.130
don't exactly mean what
we think they mean.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.040
For example, instead of
saying a straightforward word

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.480
like think, we might be tempted

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.220
to say "contemplate."

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.850
Instead of work, we say
things like, "exert oneself."

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.970
Instead of problem, we
say "problematical."

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.170
And if you think I'm exaggerating,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.331
I've heard numerous people
use the word problematical

00:01:40.331 --> 00:01:42.450
for no reason whatsoever.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:43.956
I saw a meme on social media

00:01:43.956 --> 00:01:47.317
of what looked like a wise
old man and the caption read,

00:01:47.317 --> 00:01:49.523
"Sometimes I use words I don't understand

00:01:49.523 --> 00:01:52.590
so I can sound more photosynthesis."

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.470
We do this when we
speak and when we write.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.020
A while back, I was
grading a student's paper.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.780
She used a fancy word
that didn't belong there,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.340
and I gave her the benefit of the doubt,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.590
and I asked her when I
handed the paper back

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.360
what she meant by that sentence,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.210
and I pointed at the misplaced fancy word,

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:10.447
and she literally said,

00:02:10.447 --> 00:02:11.670
"Oh, I don't know what that means,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.140
I just thought it sounded good."

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.270
Many people do this when they speak

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.540
and when they write, they
essentially play with a thesaurus

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.640
or play with a thesaurus in their head,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.657
take it too far, and the
word they end up using

00:02:22.657 --> 00:02:26.760
is too distant from a meaning
that makes sense anymore.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.220
Another reason people
may use fancier words

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.040
than they need to is for camouflage.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.070
I'm convinced that some speakers
may not really understand

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.440
exactly what they're saying just yet.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.390
There is an expression that people credit

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.580
to Richard Feynman, a physicist,

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.160
and this is a paraphrase
of what he originally said,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.377
but he deserves the credit
for the idea itself.

00:02:47.377 --> 00:02:50.430
"If you can't give a freshman-level
lecture on a subject,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.220
then you don't really understand it yet."

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.010
He said this about himself,
and I think he's right.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.230
The people who really know their stuff

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.270
are able to explain ideas

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.840
to a beginner without
oversimplifying a subject

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.330
or losing the heart of the matter.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.190
They're able to use everyday language

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:11.040
to explain even the most complex subjects.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.650
I believe people also use
big words as camouflage

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.290
because somewhere inside
themselves they may realize

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.057
that what they're saying
isn't that meaningful

00:03:18.057 --> 00:03:20.550
or important, so they use bigger words

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.800
to make the idea sound more profound

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.170
or to cover over a piece of their thinking

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.930
that is not crystal clear just yet.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.040
The lesson is this:

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.210
Don't be tempted to use
fancy-sounding vocabulary words

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.490
because you think it'll sound smart

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.560
or as a form of camouflage.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.810
It's likely that somebody listening,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:40.680
who you're speaking with,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.390
will know what the word means

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:46.020
and maybe ask a follow-up
question and then you'll be stuck.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.080
Tip number two is really
the solution to number one;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.520
use the plain English version
of words whenever possible.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.640
Do what I call the reverse
thesaurus exercise.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.320
Look for more straightforward ways

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.880
to communicate your message.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.500
My advice is to use this simple

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.110
and clear word as often as possible.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.170
Let's flip those earlier
examples and look at it again.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:11.130
Instead of contemplate, exert
oneself, or problematical,

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:14.580
use words like, think, work and problem.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.320
Everybody understands those words

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.992
and 90% of the time the fancier versions

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:22.320
of those words usually
add no additional meaning

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.270
or information to the sentence.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.610
So here's a question: do these words

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.260
sound too basic for you?

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.820
Well, let's put it to the test

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.680
when you are the listener,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.980
we'll look at a classic
example of a famous quotation.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.610
In the first version, I'll use
fancier words than necessary.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.020
You may still recognize the idea,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.360
but I think you'll agree
it does not sound as good

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.717
as the original.

00:04:43.717 --> 00:04:45.060
"Refrain from requesting

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.610
what the government can
provide for its citizens.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.610
Comparatively, request
what the populace can offer

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:51.971
the nation-state."

00:04:51.971 --> 00:04:55.020
Does that concept sound a bit familiar?

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.267
Well, let's hear the original.

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:59.161
"Ask not what your country can do for you.

00:04:59.161 --> 00:05:02.670
Ask what you can do for your country."

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.201
I'm sure you recognize
the famous quotation

00:05:04.201 --> 00:05:06.994
from John F. Kennedy from his speech.

00:05:06.994 --> 00:05:10.050
Now, let's put these two side by side

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:13.770
and look at the way Kennedy
used plain language.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.030
You'll notice that Kennedy uses

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.970
almost entirely single-syllable words.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.760
The only two-syllable word

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.530
in the entire quotation
is the word country,

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.600
and that's a word that
cannot be substituted

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.020
for a single syllable word
and still makes sense.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.510
This plain language also allowed JFK

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:33.810
to add emphasis and say
it like he meant it.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.490
When your words are clear like this

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.100
and you know that everybody
will understand you,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.398
you can lean in and communicate
the idea with confidence.

00:05:41.398 --> 00:05:43.380
If you look at my
dressed-up version again,

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.560
you can see that the fancier
words rarely add value,

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.890
meaning, or additional important
information to the message.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.830
The fancier words usually
weaken the overall impact

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:53.799
of your message.

00:05:53.799 --> 00:05:57.630
And keep in mind, JFK was
known for his intelligence.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.660
He had a powerful vocabulary
when he needed it.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.790
The legend is he was a speed-reader

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.700
and that he read every single
major newspaper each day

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.710
by the time he finished breakfast,

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:11.700
but when he spoke, he wanted
everybody to understand him.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:15.150
He didn't feel the need to
be perceived as intelligent

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.070
because he knew who he was.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.780
His focus was on being clear

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.690
so that everybody who
heard him understood him,

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.580
and that should be your goal as well.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.670
Now, you might be saying,
hold on a minute, Alex.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:27.900
Sometimes we can't do that.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.410
Sometimes there are words
that we must use to say

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.570
what needs to be said, and that's true.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.360
There are sometimes technical
words that we have to use,

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.910
otherwise we can't say
what we need to say,

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:40.770
and that's totally understandable.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.380
The tip is to keep the
rest of your sentence

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.230
simple and clear so that
the irreplaceable words

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:50.130
don't get lost in a sea
of other demanding words.

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:51.840
By the way, if you like
what you're hearing,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.720
I encourage you to find
out more about my classes

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.520
and group coaching options

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.500
and my communication coach academy.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.510
You may want to take a
look at our free class

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.660
and handout on the top five
essential communication skills

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.430
that all professionals should have.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:06.860
I'll put a link to those resources

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.060
in the description below this video.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.700
Tip three: avoid using industry jargon,

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.010
abbreviations, or acronyms.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:17.520
For example, instead of
using the abbreviation "SME,"

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.760
or S-M-E, just say
"subject matter expert."

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:24.630
Instead of saying ROI, say,
"return on investment."

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:25.950
Now let's see if you know this one:

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.268
Instead of POV, what
would you say instead?

00:07:29.268 --> 00:07:31.830
If you said "proof of
value," you're correct.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.200
However, if you said "point of view,"

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:38.070
you may also be correct
because acronyms are confusing.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.289
The speaker may intend one meaning,

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:43.020
but the listener may
assume a different meaning.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.720
The research I've seen shows
that abbreviations, jargon

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.480
and acronyms create misunderstandings.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.660
That's the opposite of
clear and confident.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.060
I witnessed this firsthand a few years ago

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.210
when I was working with a
client on an offsite retreat.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.552
As part of the weekend,
we had 40 individuals

00:07:59.552 --> 00:08:01.530
who were all part of the same division

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.870
in a large international company.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.420
These 40 people understood
each other's worlds

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:07.950
better than anyone else,

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:10.230
but when one of the
speakers went up to present

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:13.200
and filled his presentation
with technical terms,

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.210
jargon and acronyms, by the end,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:16.980
almost nobody had any idea

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.660
what he was talking about anymore.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.270
His close colleagues were
literally looking at each other

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:22.380
and shrugging their shoulders

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.810
and whispering, "I have
no idea what he's saying."

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:27.450
And here's why; anytime
someone uses an acronym,

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.970
abbreviation or jargon, the listener has

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.890
to slow down their thinking a little bit,

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:33.990
translate what it might mean,

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:35.880
and during those moments of translation,

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.852
the speaker has already
moved on in the conversation

00:08:38.852 --> 00:08:42.030
and the listener
experiences missing moments

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.040
of understanding a gap.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.020
Using jargon and acronyms like this

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.708
creates a series of gaps
in your listeners' minds

00:08:48.708 --> 00:08:51.660
and they're missing pieces of your ideas.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:53.940
These add up and listeners
won't be able to follow

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.010
what you're saying as well by the end.

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:58.620
That's gonna hurt your
credibility as a speaker

00:08:58.620 --> 00:08:59.970
and as a professional.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.770
So instead of using these shortcuts,

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:03.420
just say the words out loud,

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:04.950
define what you mean.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.270
If they're important terms,

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:09.330
so that everybody understands
and follows your meaning.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.290
Here's a summary of the
tips in this lesson.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:13.877
Don't use fancy vocabulary words

00:09:13.877 --> 00:09:17.040
for their own sake or
try to impress others.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.130
Instead, use plain
English versions of words

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:21.660
whenever possible.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.840
In the same way, avoid
using industry jargon,

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.880
abbreviations or acronyms.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.460
Plain language is clear language.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:32.460
You'll sound more confident
when you speak this way.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:33.570
As I mentioned, I encourage you

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.656
to find out more about the free resources

00:09:35.656 --> 00:09:38.295
on the website that you
can take advantage of

00:09:38.295 --> 00:09:40.830
and that class on the top five essential

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:44.070
communication skills that all
professionals should have.

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:45.300
This is a five-part series

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.070
on how to speak clearly and confidently,

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:48.720
tune in next time when you learn about how

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:52.800
to use concise sentences
to their maximum advantage.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.360
I look forward to reading
your comments below

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:57.000
about what you thought of
this video and the tips in it.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.671
Until next time, thanks, God
bless, and I will see you soon.

00:09:59.671 --> 00:10:02.671
(upbeat rock music)

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.480
You're going to learn how to speak clearly

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.650
and confidently using the
power of concise sentences.

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:13.350
As one of my consultant
friend always says,

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:15.630
Concise sounds confident.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.640
This is part two in a five-part series

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.440
on speaking clearly and confidently.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.347
Here are my three interrelated tips

00:10:21.347 --> 00:10:23.250
on how to use concise sentences

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.500
that'll make you sound fantastic.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.560
One; Avoid long, demanding sentences.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:29.970
When I coach corporate clients,

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:32.307
one of the most common
communication issues I see

00:10:32.307 --> 00:10:35.340
is that they speak in
really long sentences.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:38.970
Essentially, they string two
or three sentences together

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:42.390
into one long, redundant super sentence.

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:43.902
That's too demanding for listeners.

00:10:43.902 --> 00:10:47.010
This could be the case for
regular updates at meetings

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.590
or certainly public speaking situations.

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:51.540
I can personally relate to the tendency

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.660
to want to tell listeners
everything you know all at once.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:55.830
You might have a lot on your mind

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:58.740
and this might be your one
opportunity to share it.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.200
But long sentences make it more difficult

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.150
for your listeners to understand you.

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:04.770
It's just like as if you're reading

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:07.080
and you come across a sentence in a book

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:09.360
that goes for five or six lines long

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.660
or a paragraph that stretches
over two or more pages.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:14.030
That can overwhelm readers

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:17.280
and in the same way
listeners need a break.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.750
If listeners cannot follow you

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.220
because you're long-winded,

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:21.690
you won't create the impression

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.580
that you are clear and confident.

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:25.271
That will wear your listeners out,

00:11:25.271 --> 00:11:28.770
but the question is, how
can you avoid long sentences

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:30.262
when there's so much to say?

00:11:30.262 --> 00:11:33.090
My advice is to keep those long sentences,

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:36.126
but find a way to break
them in a natural spot

00:11:36.126 --> 00:11:39.750
into two or three shorter sentences.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:41.490
Now, this is the right advice,

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:43.050
but it's easier said than done.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:45.990
Many clients are used to
speaking in long sentences

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.460
and they usually struggle at first

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:50.040
to get into the rhythm
of shorter sentences.

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.050
So let's talk about tip number two;

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.784
practice speaking in bullet points.

00:11:54.784 --> 00:11:57.750
Now, speaking as if you're
stating bullet points

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.090
feels stiff and wooden at first,

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:02.580
but trust this exercise,
it's just practice.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:05.010
If you train yourself to sound this way,

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:07.320
you'll be more clear and
confident in actual meetings

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:08.790
and in real conversations.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.728
I learned the technique
I'm about to show you

00:12:10.728 --> 00:12:13.830
from actual clients that I've worked with.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.800
I've noticed that my clients
who spoke the most clearly

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.300
and sounded the most confident

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.700
spoke in short sentences.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.610
To me, it sounded like they
were speaking in bullet points,

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:26.040
almost as if they had a PowerPoint slide

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.010
to refer to, and they sounded fantastic.

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:30.270
Here's how they do it.

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.940
They make one short
statement after another,

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:35.490
but each one is followed by a brief pause

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:37.860
where the period goes on the slide.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.390
They don't say "um," or "uh,"

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:40.620
between the ideas,

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:43.560
they just pause silently at
the end of each sentence.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:45.187
They also don't connect their sentences

00:12:45.187 --> 00:12:48.060
with words like "and," or "so."

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:50.040
Be on the lookout for those fill-in words.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.064
The top two words that
create run-on sentences

00:12:53.064 --> 00:12:54.990
are "And," and "So."

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:56.799
My clear and confident
clients don't use them.

00:12:56.799 --> 00:12:59.250
When they reach a period
at the end of a thought,

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:02.070
they just pause silently
for about a second.

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:05.194
The rhythm goes like this:
Short sentence, pause;

00:13:05.194 --> 00:13:09.480
short sentence, pause;
short sentence, pause.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:10.680
As if they're working their way

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.810
through bullet points on a slide.

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:15.576
So let me demonstrate a
before and after example.

00:13:15.576 --> 00:13:18.750
I'll read one long
connected sentence first

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:19.950
and then I'll fix it.

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:22.657
Here's the long demanding version.

00:13:22.657 --> 00:13:25.380
"I have several steps to
accomplish by the end of the day

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:27.540
and I have to outline their proposal

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.100
and run that by the copy editor

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:30.810
and then get the final version approved,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.120
so I'd likely then add some images

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:34.890
and illustrations to bring it to life

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:36.480
so it looks professional and polished

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:39.180
instead of looking like a
rough outline of ideas."

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:42.060
That's clearly a demanding
sentence for listeners.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:45.090
That's line after line with no break.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:46.230
Now let's fix it.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:47.190
Here's what it sounds like

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:50.040
when I pretend I'm
speaking in bullet points

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:52.567
with tiny pauses in between.

00:13:52.567 --> 00:13:54.360
"I have several steps to accomplish.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.720
By the end of the day I have
to outline the proposal.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.660
I'll run that by the copy editor.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:03.090
I'll then get the final version approved.

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:04.830
I'll likely add some images

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:07.252
and illustrations to bring it to life.

00:14:07.252 --> 00:14:10.080
We want this to look
professional and polished

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:13.320
instead of looking like
a rough outline of ideas.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:15.570
Now, neither version is Shakespeare.

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.940
I'll grant you that, but
to make it sound better,

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:21.000
all I did was turn that one long sentence

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.580
into six shorter sentences.

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:26.640
The exact same information
is in both versions.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:28.470
Now, if you wanted to, you can rewind

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:30.773
and practice both versions
to hear the difference

00:14:30.773 --> 00:14:34.950
and feel the cadence or
the rhythm for yourself.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:35.783
For your own communication,

00:14:35.783 --> 00:14:38.640
break those long sentences into two

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.710
or perhaps three sentences.

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:42.093
Pretend you're working your way

00:14:42.093 --> 00:14:44.730
through bullet points on a slide.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:45.990
And the more you practice this,

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:48.600
the more clear and confident you'll sound.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:50.010
I was going over this tip

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:52.676
with a client in my Communication
Coach Academy recently.

00:14:52.676 --> 00:14:55.320
He asked how I would translate this tip

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.440
for one-on-one or group conversations,

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.030
and here's what I told them.

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:02.730
Use concise sentences
and conversations by,

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:03.810
tip number three:

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.450
Using short talking turns.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:10.015
Conversations should be a
dialogue, not a monologue.

00:15:10.015 --> 00:15:12.390
When it's your turn to
talk in a conversation,

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:15.180
keep it short, boil down
what you want to say

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:16.389
to a sentence or two.

00:15:16.389 --> 00:15:19.650
When you speak in short
sentences in one-on-one

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:22.808
or group situations, you'll
absolutely sound clearer

00:15:22.808 --> 00:15:24.060
and more confident

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:27.390
because you're speaking in
clear bite-sized chunks.

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:29.040
Some people resist this advice

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.720
because they wanna make sure

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.430
that once it's their talking turn,

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:34.260
they say everything they came to say,

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:35.700
and I totally understand that.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.647
But I have found the opposite to be true.

00:15:37.647 --> 00:15:39.930
When they use long talking turns,

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:43.410
they're more likely to get
interrupted with questions.

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:45.541
It's unlikely that most
other professionals

00:15:45.541 --> 00:15:47.816
want to hear all your thoughts

00:15:47.816 --> 00:15:50.790
on all your topics on your list that day.

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:53.670
They want to have a back
and forth conversation.

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:55.650
It's 100% natural for them

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:59.610
to want to respond to
each idea in real time.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.530
When we're using short talking turns,

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.560
the conversation's much more
satisfying and effective.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:07.404
All of the same good
information will come out.

00:16:07.404 --> 00:16:09.581
It just may come out in
smaller digestible bites

00:16:09.581 --> 00:16:12.090
as you go back and forth.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:13.290
This is all the more important

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:15.690
when you're speaking with people above you

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:18.780
in the organization or
people with high status,

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:21.750
they want you to get to the point quickly.

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:23.471
If you have a high stakes conversation

00:16:23.471 --> 00:16:25.260
on your calendar like this,

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:27.060
then prepare yourself ahead of time

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:30.867
to take short talking turns
and make sure it's a dialogue.

00:16:30.867 --> 00:16:34.170
Don't attempt to monologue
with a long talking turn

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.000
with high status people in business.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.215
They want you to lead with a bottom line

00:16:38.215 --> 00:16:40.074
and then you can both talk about it.

00:16:40.074 --> 00:16:44.190
In summary, concise sounds confident.

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:47.370
Here's a recap of how to
use concise sentences:

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:50.040
Avoid long demanding sentences,

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:51.575
practice speaking in bullet points

00:16:51.575 --> 00:16:55.170
and use short talking turns.

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:57.600
If you're benefiting from
this series of videos,

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:00.150
I encourage you to find
out more about my classes

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:02.790
and group coaching at my
Communication Coach Academy.

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:04.080
There's also a full page

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:06.630
of free resources including a free class

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:09.090
on the top five essential
communication skills

00:17:09.090 --> 00:17:10.710
that all professionals should have.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:13.136
I'll put links to those in the
description below the video.

00:17:13.136 --> 00:17:15.570
Be sure to take a look at part three

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:17.970
in this five-part series
on how to speak clearly

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:22.230
and confidently by using
identifiable talking points.

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:24.300
I'll show you how to do
that in the next video.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:27.691
Until then, thanks, God
bless, and I'll see you soon.

00:17:27.691 --> 00:17:30.691
(upbeat rock music)

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:33.450
You're going to learn how to speak clearly

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:35.310
and confidently by designing your message

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:37.732
with logical main points.

00:17:37.732 --> 00:17:39.300
If you're following along,

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.820
this is part three in a five-part series

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:44.310
on speaking clearly and confidently.

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:46.650
Here's the bottom line for this video.

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:48.660
Clear and confident speakers communicate

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:52.190
in logical main points or talking points.

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:55.770
In other words, they communicate
in an orderly fashion.

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:57.420
For these tips, I'm going to assume

00:17:57.420 --> 00:17:59.100
you're in a professional situation,

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:00.780
like a team meeting.

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:02.809
You could use these tips
to prepare a message

00:18:02.809 --> 00:18:06.120
or as part of a Q&amp;A in a group situation,

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:09.780
so picture yourself talking
in a group for these tips.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:12.480
Tip one, the easiest way
to add a sense of logic

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:16.254
and structure to your message
is to give a brief preview,

00:18:16.254 --> 00:18:18.990
and then number your main points allowed.

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:20.940
For example, you might say you wanted

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.610
to talk about three important steps.

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:26.220
First, let's look for
a way to boost revenue.

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:28.762
Second, we'll explore how to cut costs,

00:18:28.762 --> 00:18:32.610
and third, we must reach new customers.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:34.710
My previous statement
there was simply to say,

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:37.398
I want to talk about
three important steps.

00:18:37.398 --> 00:18:39.997
Listeners now know that
you have three points

00:18:39.997 --> 00:18:42.390
and then you'd number your points

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:43.410
as you say them.

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:45.000
When you say first, second, and third,

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.160
these are called signposts.

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:49.457
You're numbering your
points to make it easier

00:18:49.457 --> 00:18:51.660
for your listeners to hear the logic

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.790
and structure of your message.

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.850
That adds a lot of clarity
to what you've said.

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:57.683
But you'll notice

00:18:57.683 --> 00:19:00.060
that it also makes you
sound incredibly confident

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:01.290
in what you're saying.

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:03.210
You sound like you've thought about this

00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:04.860
and you know what you're talking about.

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:07.783
In contrast, if you don't
literally say first, second,

00:19:07.783 --> 00:19:10.230
and third, when you get to those points,

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:12.953
your listeners won't
hear that extra clarity.

00:19:12.953 --> 00:19:14.100
It could sound to them

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:16.260
like you're just listening random items,

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:19.120
but by adding those signposts,
first, second, third,

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:21.990
you sound more prepared and credible.

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:24.600
I'll do another example,
and on this first version,

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.850
I won't use a preview or a signpost.

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:29.045
And then I'll redo it
by adding those elements

00:19:29.045 --> 00:19:31.947
and you tell me which one
sounds more clear and confident.

00:19:31.947 --> 00:19:35.820
Picture yourself saying each
of these in a group meeting.

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:37.177
Here's the first example.

00:19:37.177 --> 00:19:40.080
"Let's identify other
departments we could work with.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:41.820
We'll meet with them to discuss our idea

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:44.220
and we can collaborate
to draft a proposal."

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:45.780
Now, there's nothing wrong with that.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:48.360
It's already clear as it is,
but listen to the difference

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:51.697
when I say it with a
preview and signposts.

00:19:51.697 --> 00:19:54.540
"I see three next steps for this project.

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:57.298
First, let's identify other
departments we could work with.

00:19:57.298 --> 00:20:00.030
Second, we'll meet with
them to discuss our idea

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:03.420
and third, we can collaborate
to draft a proposal."

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:05.460
Both of these are passable messages,

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.950
but most people would agree
that the second example

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:09.360
has that extra edge

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:12.180
that makes it sound more like leadership.

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.040
Adding a preview and signposts

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:16.110
makes the message sound official.

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:17.520
As The Rock used to say,

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:20.070
if you're picking up what I'm
throwing down in this series,

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:21.690
I encourage you to find out more

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:23.970
about my Communication Coach Academy.

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:25.740
We have over 20 classes

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:28.680
and we offer live group
coaching on top of that.

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:31.530
There's also a page full
of entirely free resources

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:32.460
and a free class

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:35.190
on the top five essential
communication skills

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:36.510
for professionals.

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:38.720
I'll put links to that and other resources

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:40.560
in the description below the video.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.290
Tip number two, if you
have a persuasive message,

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:47.127
use the problem, solution,
benefits main points.

00:20:47.127 --> 00:20:49.320
In professional situations,

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:52.800
we are often asking our
colleagues, supervisors,

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:56.010
or clients for something
at the end of our message.

00:20:56.010 --> 00:21:00.510
We call this an ask, a
call to action, or a CTA.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:02.263
Every couple of weeks we're asking

00:21:02.263 --> 00:21:05.820
for funding, support
or collaborative help.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:07.617
When we do, we must build a case

00:21:07.617 --> 00:21:11.430
and persuade people to join our cause.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.590
When you're sharing that
type of message, the best way

00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:15.690
to organize your main points is

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:18.420
in problem-solution benefit order.

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:19.320
It works the same

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:21.810
as the first, second, third
signpost we talked about,

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:25.260
except instead of filling in
first, second, and third points

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:27.720
with whatever topics you're talking about,

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:29.190
you're gonna fill them in

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:31.350
with the problem you're
there to talk about

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:34.290
or the need, the solution, or your plan,

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:35.790
and the benefits.

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:37.260
It must be in that order

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.960
to be an effective persuasive message.

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:42.840
Here's an example of a message
about asking your supervisor

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:45.193
or team to hire an additional person

00:21:45.193 --> 00:21:47.850
for an important upcoming project.

00:21:47.850 --> 00:21:50.190
First, as you know, our key issue

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:53.010
is that we lost a couple
of people last quarter

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:56.160
and we're heading into our
next project shorthanded.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:58.440
Second, because of this,
I recommend we hire

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:00.810
at least one additional
person who can help us

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:04.060
with the instructional design
aspects of our project.

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:05.820
Third, the upside of this

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:07.560
is it will free up the rest of us

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:09.840
to increase the quality of our work

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:12.690
because we're not trying
to pull double duty.

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:14.640
A couple of pointers on this message.

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.216
You could use the actual keywords;

00:22:17.216 --> 00:22:20.880
problem, solution, and benefit
to express your message,

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:22.220
or you can substitute words

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:24.930
that mean almost the
same thing like I did.

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:28.950
For example, I use the word
issue, recommend, and upside;

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:30.780
instead of problem, solution, benefit.

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:31.800
There are a few ways

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:34.830
to communicate the same logical structure.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:39.150
If you want to say issues,
obstacles, trouble or hangups,

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:41.010
instead of using the
actual word "problem,"

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:42.210
that's up to you.

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:44.010
If you want to use words like plan,

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:46.140
recommendation or suggestion

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:47.490
rather than the word, "solution,"

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:48.807
those are great substitutes.

00:22:48.807 --> 00:22:51.856
You can use whatever words
fit your message best.

00:22:51.856 --> 00:22:54.750
Just be sure you're
structuring your message

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:57.960
around a foundation of
problem, solution, benefits;

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:01.980
in that order, and you use
some unmistakable keywords

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:04.410
to signal those three points.

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:06.090
I'd also like to point out here

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:08.940
that I use signposts for
each of those points.

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:10.650
I said first, second, and third.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:13.627
In addition to saying
issue, recommend and upside.

00:23:13.627 --> 00:23:16.770
I almost always recommend using signposts.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:19.170
When in doubt, use signposts.

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:21.480
They usually help and they never hurt,

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.390
but in some cases using a
signpost is not necessary

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:27.030
if your keywords are crystal clear.

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:28.508
Tip number three is a variation

00:23:28.508 --> 00:23:31.230
on a persuasive message design.

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:33.450
For some messages, three points structured

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:37.110
around problem, cause,
solution may be a better fit

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:39.960
for your message than
problem, solution, benefit.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:41.550
And there are really no ways to tell

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:44.820
ahead of time which design is best

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:46.440
in your particular situation

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:48.030
for your message and your audience.

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:49.500
That choice is up to you.

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:51.490
But in my experience
working with my clients,

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:55.050
I find that problem,
cause, solution main points

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:57.810
tend to work really well
with an internal audience;

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:00.450
like your team or perhaps your supervisor.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:01.530
Most of my clients agree

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:04.440
that problem, solution,
benefits main points

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:07.020
work equally well with
either an internal audience

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:08.940
like those people that I just mentioned

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:12.510
or an external audience,
like a potential client.

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:15.420
Here's an example of what problem
cause solution main points

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:18.150
sound like for a brief message.

00:24:18.150 --> 00:24:19.380
And for the sake of variety,

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:22.110
I will not use signposts in this example

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:25.278
so you can see how it sounds
just with the key words.

00:24:25.278 --> 00:24:27.240
Our biggest problem currently

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:28.830
is that we have been losing more clients

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:31.530
because of attrition
than we thought we would.

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:32.993
The cause of this has been the lack

00:24:32.993 --> 00:24:36.810
of a coherent onboarding
and orientation process

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:38.250
for new clients.

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:39.390
The solution I recommend

00:24:39.390 --> 00:24:41.310
is to create a new onboarding process

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:44.105
that includes a balance of
face-to-face interaction

00:24:44.105 --> 00:24:47.640
and email follow-up and equal measure.

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:48.720
Some people I've worked with

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:50.340
are hesitant to use keywords

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:53.670
that are as deliberate as
problem, cause, solution.

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:55.160
Like before, you can substitute other

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:57.360
keywords and phrases for these.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:00.150
Just make sure your keywords stand out

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:01.350
and are unmistakable

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:04.350
so your listeners know
exactly what point you're on.

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:06.660
There are other ways
to design your message

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:09.240
around logical points, but
these are the top three ways

00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:10.824
that you've probably already heard

00:25:10.824 --> 00:25:13.470
in most professional settings.

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:15.210
If you can learn to speak like this

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:17.880
and make it a habit, you'll
sound 10 times more clear

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:21.053
and confident for every
important message you share.

